WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.168 --> 00:00:01.800
- Egg race.

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:03.000
- Egg race?
- Yeah.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.850
- [Hannah] Okay, one, two, three, go.

00:00:07.618 --> 00:00:08.701
Whoa!
- I win!

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:12.020
- [Hannah] That was amazing!

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:12.925
- [Boy] I win!

00:00:12.925 --> 00:00:16.008
(lighthearted music)

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:21.880
Hi, I'm Hannah and I'm
the World's OK-est Mom.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:25.300
Did you know that single-mother
households make up 23%

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.940
of the U.S. population?

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.340
That's a lot of single moms,

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:29.780
and I wanna know how they do it.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:32.530
So, Matt is leaving on a
business trip for a whole week

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:33.940
and I'm gonna take that time

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.200
to see what my life
would be like if I were

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.410
to become a single mom right now.

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:40.480
I'm a little bit nervous,
but I'm gonna try it!

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.730
Seven days, two kids,
one mom, let's do it.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:47.550
So, Matt is gonna be
gone for one full week

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:49.840
and in that full week
it's gonna be just me.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.710
And in order to make this
week as authentic as possible,

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.510
I did a little research on single moms

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:57.250
and I found out that in
2011 the median income

00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:59.610
for married mothers was $80,000.

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:01.810
For single moms, it was four times less.

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:05.330
So, what I did was calculate my income

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:07.340
as the sole income of our household,

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:10.160
and after factoring out rent and daycare

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.700
and insurance and all those expenses,

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:14.890
I divided what I had left by four

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:17.470
and gave myself that amount
to spend for that week.

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:20.030
Right off the bat, that
meant that my babysitter,

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:22.700
who I pay to pick up the
kids every day from school,

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:25.570
was off the table; I could
not afford her for that week.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:27.230
Now, I hire her to pick up the kids

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.440
so that I don't have to leave
work early to pick them up,

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:30.880
and she just has them for a little bit,

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:34.080
but that little bit
was way too much for me

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.140
to even have any money
left over for that week.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:37.870
And even further, I decided not

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:39.770
to rely on my family
for that week because,

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:42.850
for a lot of people, they
don't have family nearby,

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:45.100
or they don't have family
members who stay home

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:48.490
and are able to help out with
the kids full-time for free.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:50.920
- Hi.
- This is your big break.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:53.200
- Your big break.
(laughs)

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:54.033
- My big break.

00:01:54.033 --> 00:01:56.740
I can watch all the Forensic Files I want.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:57.700
We said our goodbyes,

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.550
and I realized right as
he walked out the door,

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:02.070
I needed to go to Target.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.840
So, it is eight o'clock in the evening,

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:08.150
and we are going to
Target, all of us together.

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:11.510
Aww, Jackson hit his face scootering.

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:13.900
I have both kids, it's past their bedtime.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.010
Usually I would leave
them at home and run out,

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.240
but we need some stuff for tomorrow

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.310
and I have to take them with.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.250
Now, parents know when you go to Target

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.800
with your kids, it's
like walking a tightrope.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.260
Target is not only a
parents' shopping paradise,

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:29.720
it's a kids' shopping paradise.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:32.010
So it's just like the
worst of both worlds.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:33.710
Come on, let's keep going Jackson.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.660
Jackson, we're only here
for a couple things, okay?

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:40.210
I needed two things at Target,
and so I was determined

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:43.870
to go in and out with two things.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:47.580
It is 9:30, we got a little sidetracked

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.130
at the, video game section.

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:55.130
I had to, shoot down a
couple hopes and dreams.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:58.373
We're running a little late,
pretty behind for tonight.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:02.070
Definitely not doing this again.

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.850
I was just flat on my back
exhausted after that Target run.

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:09.710
Today was pretty tough,
Target was difficult.

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:11.930
Now would be about the
time that I'd pass bedtime

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:15.320
off to Matt so that I can relax,

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.833
but it's just me so we're gonna do.

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:19.923
Cuddles?

00:03:19.923 --> 00:03:21.150
- Cuddles!

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:22.130
- Cuddles?

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:22.963
- Yeah.

00:03:22.963 --> 00:03:24.880
- Good first day, huh?

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:28.222
- [Boy] Yeah, it's mama day.

00:03:28.222 --> 00:03:29.156
- Mama day.

00:03:29.156 --> 00:03:29.989
- Mommy day.

00:03:29.989 --> 00:03:31.003
- Goodnight guys.

00:03:31.003 --> 00:03:32.290
- Goodnight.
- Goodnight.

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:34.480
- This morning was rough.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.930
What are you shoving in the mail slot?

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:38.480
To say the least.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:40.670
Let's get socks on.

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:42.410
Come on Wyatt, let's get socks on.

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:45.100
So we got Wyatt dropped
off finally, very late,

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:46.750
and now we're heading to my work

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:49.930
and Jackson is on spring
break, so he's coming

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:52.110
with me to the office because

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:53.410
I don't know what else to do with him.

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:57.420
If you are a working parent and
you know the answer to this,

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:59.210
leave it in the comments.

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:04.080
What the heck do you do with
your kid on school vacations?

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:05.910
Where do you put them?

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:08.760
'Cause Jackson had one
day left of spring break

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:10.900
and I did not know what to do with him.

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:13.865
I can't afford a sitter all
day to come stay with you.

00:04:13.865 --> 00:04:15.840
Yeah, I didn't know what to do with him,

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.610
so I just hauled him in the
car and took him to work.

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:20.650
So, as we're walking in to the office,

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:24.570
I realize that I forgot
to fix Jackson's hair,

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:26.400
and he dressed himself.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.490
Jackson, you're rockin' it!

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:30.830
You look awesome!
- Thank you.

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:33.810
- Jackson noticed and so
kindly pointed out to me,

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:35.650
as kids often do.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:37.920
- Mama, you were wearing
that all nighttime.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.374
- This shirt, yes I did sleep in it.

00:04:40.374 --> 00:04:41.370
(laughs)

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:44.040
It's like the parent
equivalent of walk of shame.

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:46.010
But, it was clean when I slept in it,

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:48.220
and I was clean when I put it on,

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:50.740
so it's still clean the next day.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:53.100
Jackson was so easy at my office that day.

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:56.880
We had a great day, got
home, got ready for bed,

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:59.850
and then I had a choice to make.

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:03.410
I really should get up and clean,

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:06.860
and do some dishes and laundry and stuff,

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:10.180
but I'm so tired right now,

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:12.670
I just wanna sleep on this couch.

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:16.680
So I think I'm gonna put
everything off for one more day.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:18.270
Now, both my kids sleep through the night,

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:19.810
so it's very rare that we have

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:22.030
middle-of-the-night
incidents, but of course,

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:23.520
that night something happened.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.050
Jackson has a little cut on his lip.

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:29.080
While he was sleeping,
it caught on his pillow

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:33.860
and it bled a little
and I do not do blood.

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:36.210
That is not my thing.

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:39.340
That is something I delegate,
but I'm gonna handle it

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:42.493
and clean it up and get
Jackson back to bed.

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:45.430
You're brave buddy.

00:05:45.430 --> 00:05:48.360
Okay, we're in the car on our way

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:51.840
to get these forms I need,
this paperwork I need.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:52.944
- [Boy] Target!

00:05:52.944 --> 00:05:55.160
- We finally, no we are
not going to Target,

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:56.890
I learned my lesson.
(laughs)

00:05:56.890 --> 00:05:59.330
Then I have to get gas.

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:00.750
I have a call that I'm gonna have to take

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:02.640
in the car on the way, I'm just a mess.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:05.350
So after all the errands,
I ran into that same issue

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:07.300
of what do I do with my kids?

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:09.420
All right, well I've
just completely given up

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:11.590
on the idea of going to the office today.

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:14.860
It is one o'clock and they're hungry,

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:16.090
so they need to be fed.

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:18.660
I think we're gonna
try to do a drive-thru,

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:20.750
which they'll be really excited about

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:22.440
'cause we don't do that very often.

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:25.220
And it's reasonable, it's
within my budget for this week,

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:28.430
so that's what we're gonna do.

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:29.513
I would love a nap.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:32.000
And a bath.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:34.150
I decided to work from
home the rest of the day

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:37.830
because, like I said, my
company is very flexible

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:39.110
with the fact that I'm a parent.

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:40.610
They're very understanding

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:43.290
and it's not something
I like to do regularly,

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:44.510
but it's something that they allow me

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:47.020
to do if I absolutely need to.

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:49.390
So while I had the luxury
of working from home,

00:06:49.390 --> 00:06:51.040
actually getting the work done

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:54.000
was a whole different story because.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:58.600
So rainy, the kids can
not play outside today.

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:01.070
Can you guys just give me five minutes

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:03.143
to get my work done, please?

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:07.273
Please, Jackson, please?

00:07:08.751 --> 00:07:12.030
I feel like I'm going to lose
it because I'm just trying

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:15.620
to get a little work done and I hear

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:18.810
banging and crashing in
their room, and they have.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:24.040
Managed to completely empty their closet

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:26.650
and dump everything on the floor.

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:29.640
And now I have this on
top of all the other mess.

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:31.690
It was a frustrating day.

00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:33.360
Day four,

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:34.620
was a great day.

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:39.310
Started off with Wyatt not
cooperating with me at all.

00:07:39.310 --> 00:07:40.660
You're not listening to me.

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:44.315
I asked you to sit down
at the table, please.

00:07:44.315 --> 00:07:49.315
Wyatt?

00:07:49.817 --> 00:07:50.650
Wyatt.

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:54.220
I realized that in my rush
to get ready this morning

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:56.740
and accessorize with this necklace

00:07:56.740 --> 00:08:00.250
and actually get dressed,
I forgot my purse.

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:04.000
I'm such a dummy, like I'm the
biggest dodo on the planet.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.810
Who forgets their purse?

00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:08.590
I'm really nervous about
driving without a license.

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:10.387
Nick is gonna drive my car.

00:08:10.387 --> 00:08:11.860
(sighs)
- Yes.

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:14.660
- 'Cause he's the best
friend in the entire world,

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:17.050
and this is what friends are for

00:08:17.050 --> 00:08:22.050
and it takes a village,
especially this week.

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:23.294
- I hear that.

00:08:23.294 --> 00:08:24.320
(laughs)

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:26.777
- We are home!

00:08:26.777 --> 00:08:29.360
(Wyatt crying)

00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:33.640
So that night, it was Wyatt
versus chicken nuggets.

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:36.160
He's not happy about my dinner choice

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:37.540
that I threw in the oven today.

00:08:37.540 --> 00:08:38.850
(Wyatt crying)

00:08:38.850 --> 00:08:41.550
But, you know what, he can deal with it,

00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:43.860
because that's what I'm
making, because it's easy

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:47.930
and that's what we have and that's it.

00:08:47.930 --> 00:08:49.219
Do you like chicken nuggets?

00:08:49.219 --> 00:08:50.052
- Yeah.

00:08:50.052 --> 00:08:53.235
- Wyatt, you'll learn
to love chicken nuggets.

00:08:53.235 --> 00:08:55.900
He actually likes them,

00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:58.640
he just doesn't like them
'cause I said I'm making them.

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:01.600
So, day five, I realized
that I was starting

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:03.700
to get a little bit touched out.

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:06.117
Now this is something that
happens when you have kids

00:09:06.117 --> 00:09:07.840
and it's something you have to adjust to

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:10.850
is just being constantly touched.

00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:14.500
And without Matt there to
kind of take half the touches,

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:18.270
it was all me, I just had
two kids piling on top of me.

00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:20.550
It's like one will plank on my chest,

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:23.940
and one takes the arm,
there is no other way.

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:25.540
And they will fight each other

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:29.370
for more real estate of my body to touch.

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:32.240
So day five, it was starting
to wear on me a little bit.

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:34.600
I decided to start the
day with a little treat,

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:36.320
we had a little money in our budget

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:39.250
to get something inexpensive,
to go out to eat,

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:40.670
so I took them out for breakfast.

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:43.810
So we got bagels and
I got a little coffee,

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:45.410
and I took them to this coffee place

00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:47.250
that I know has some toys where they can

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:49.340
kind of run around and build blocks,

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:52.130
and do a bunch of stuff
and get out some energy.

00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:55.769
Because, we had to make
another Target trip that day.

00:09:55.769 --> 00:09:57.420
(blocks crashing)

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:01.100
So we got in and Wyatt,
because of his age,

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:03.330
kids that age will get
fixated on something

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:05.280
and just not be able to let go of it.

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:08.955
So, that Target run, it was I want boxers.

00:10:08.955 --> 00:10:09.788
(laughs)

00:10:09.788 --> 00:10:12.110
You wear a diaper still kid, come on.

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:14.360
It started with I want boxers

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:16.100
and then throughout the trip it turned

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:19.700
into I want boxers and then

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:21.506
by the time we were checking out it was.

00:10:21.506 --> 00:10:24.140
(Wyatt crying)

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:27.259
And then in the parking
lot, it just got even worse.

00:10:27.259 --> 00:10:28.279
(Wyatt crying)

00:10:28.279 --> 00:10:30.435
All right, let's go to the car.

00:10:30.435 --> 00:10:32.768
All right, come on, come on.

00:10:34.330 --> 00:10:38.650
My approach to parenting
and melt-downs is always

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:40.530
to treat them with love

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:42.460
because I feel like, at
Wyatt's age, he's three

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:44.370
and he, he doesn't understand.

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:46.710
But, sometimes it just doesn't work

00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:48.930
and sometimes nothing works.

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:50.420
You just have to ride it out.

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:52.670
And a lot of the time I see parents,

00:10:52.670 --> 00:10:54.700
you know, trying to get their errands done

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:56.480
with a kid melting down in the cart,

00:10:56.480 --> 00:10:57.920
and other people are looking at them,

00:10:57.920 --> 00:10:59.910
like, "why don't you do
something about that,"

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:02.500
but sometimes doing
something makes it worse.

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:05.930
That was one of those
meltdowns where I just had to.

00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:07.251
Not engage.

00:11:07.251 --> 00:11:09.911
(Wyatt crying)

00:11:09.911 --> 00:11:10.756
- [Jackson] Wyatt, would you please stop?

00:11:10.756 --> 00:11:12.056
- I don't know what to do.

00:11:16.440 --> 00:11:18.680
Now, I chose to film a few seconds of it,

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:21.090
because I feel like
it's something very real

00:11:21.090 --> 00:11:22.900
that a lot of parents experience.

00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:25.000
So, while it was tough on Wyatt,

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:27.390
and it's not a fun thing to watch,

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:29.480
it was also really tough on me.

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:32.110
It is taking all of my patience

00:11:33.250 --> 00:11:36.403
in my entire body to not
completely snap right now.

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:42.810
So the morning of day six was pretty much

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:45.130
the lowest point of the week for me.

00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:48.620
So, I don't know if you
guys can tell from my eyes,

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:52.633
but I kind of had a little
meltdown last night.

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:56.930
Kind of like, kind of lost it.

00:11:56.930 --> 00:12:00.030
Didn't shower, so I'm
trying to last-minute shower

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:02.833
this morning, boys are fighting out there.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:06.810
But it just all got
overwhelming last night

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:07.963
and I was just, like,

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:12.560
I don't, it's only a week, you know.

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:16.087
I should be able to do this
all by myself for a week.

00:12:16.087 --> 00:12:20.910
But it's just, there are
not enough hours in the day

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:24.810
to get my job done and
then pick up the kids

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:26.410
and do the rest of it.

00:12:26.410 --> 00:12:28.020
There are just not
enough hours in the day.

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:31.370
And I need help, it's become so apparent.

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:33.503
They don't.
(sighs)

00:12:36.415 --> 00:12:38.990
I don't know how to even
put this, I'm so tired.

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:42.200
I have a harder time getting them

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:47.200
to do stuff than Matt does,
and it's become very apparent.

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:50.920
So I decided that day to
make a concerted effort

00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:53.200
to do better with the kids.

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:54.668
What are we doing?

00:12:54.668 --> 00:12:56.400
(children talking)

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:58.810
We're playing video games.

00:12:58.810 --> 00:13:00.071
- Yeah, I love playing games.

00:13:00.071 --> 00:13:01.523
(Wyatt talking)
- Because, sometimes.

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:07.460
Yes, sometimes you need to
practice a little self care

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:12.590
and distract your kids with
video games while you shower.

00:13:12.590 --> 00:13:13.543
You're doing great!

00:13:14.425 --> 00:13:17.001
- I'm not playing yet!
(Hannah laughs)

00:13:17.001 --> 00:13:19.200
- I mean, some parents
are against video games,

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:22.520
but, to be honest, they don't
really know how to play them.

00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:24.270
So they just, like, click a couple buttons

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:25.470
and they think they're doing something

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:26.950
and they're really happy with it.

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:29.220
How do you feel about this Wyatt, ready?

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:30.612
Ready?

00:13:30.612 --> 00:13:32.989
(childish babbling)

00:13:32.989 --> 00:13:36.040
- [Jackson] What?
(all laughing)

00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:41.040
So, day seven, last day, was a good day.

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:45.640
It was not raining, so we
whipped out the boys little,

00:13:45.640 --> 00:13:47.440
land speeder that they can drive,

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:50.240
and I sat outside and
let them drive around

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:51.710
for a really long time.

00:13:51.710 --> 00:13:54.310
And then I also got them
this really awesome toy.

00:13:54.310 --> 00:13:57.670
We have a rare moment of peace and quiet.

00:13:57.670 --> 00:14:00.969
I got the boys some rocks.

00:14:00.969 --> 00:14:02.830
- I'm loving this!
- I got one!

00:14:02.830 --> 00:14:04.470
- Oh, you got something!

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:07.970
They're digging for crystals,
it was this little rock kit,

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:08.970
and it was about $6.

00:14:11.380 --> 00:14:13.650
Best money I've ever spent.

00:14:13.650 --> 00:14:16.300
So I had them put on their
PJ's and get ready for bed,

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:19.663
and right before they went
to bed, Matt came home!

00:14:19.663 --> 00:14:20.627
- Hey!

00:14:20.627 --> 00:14:22.877
- [Hannah] Look who's here!

00:14:23.775 --> 00:14:24.858
Daddy's here!

00:14:26.011 --> 00:14:27.560
All right!

00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:29.754
I, we had a great week.

00:14:29.754 --> 00:14:31.610
- Hey!
- I will see you next week.

00:14:31.610 --> 00:14:33.295
Goodbye, guys.

00:14:33.295 --> 00:14:34.623
- Bye.
- Bye.

00:14:37.256 --> 00:14:39.220
(laughing)
- Oh, man, I'm ready to,

00:14:39.220 --> 00:14:41.060
I'm ready to go to Mexico.

00:14:41.060 --> 00:14:42.406
I did miss you.

00:14:42.406 --> 00:14:43.386
- [Matt] Aww, just a little bit?

00:14:43.386 --> 00:14:44.635
- I'm glad you're back.

00:14:44.635 --> 00:14:45.468
- Hi.

00:14:46.390 --> 00:14:47.790
- Thank you for coming back.

00:14:49.166 --> 00:14:49.999
- [Matt] I almost didn't.

00:14:49.999 --> 00:14:52.290
(laughs)
- I don't blame you.

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:53.660
So what did I learn this week?

00:14:53.660 --> 00:14:56.160
I tried to make things as
hard on myself as possible

00:14:56.160 --> 00:14:58.560
for this whole week, but I fully recognize

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:00.320
that I only scratched the surface.

00:15:00.320 --> 00:15:03.080
I'm doing this already in
a career that I've achieved

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:06.370
because I had a partner help
me while raising our children.

00:15:06.370 --> 00:15:08.140
I'm already at an advantage with that

00:15:08.140 --> 00:15:10.250
and that's something I
couldn't change or take away.

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:13.130
While I was juggling my job and both kids,

00:15:13.130 --> 00:15:16.660
I realized that most
single moms have two jobs

00:15:16.660 --> 00:15:19.330
or two jobs and they're
going to school full time,

00:15:19.330 --> 00:15:20.970
or they're just going to school full time

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:23.070
and trying to figure out
how to pay for everything.

00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:26.130
I didn't even remotely experience

00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:28.200
how difficult it actually is.

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:31.500
So by me doing this for a week,
and you guys watching this,

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:33.970
I hope that there's a
little more understanding

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:38.500
of how hard it is and how hard
it can be for single moms.

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:41.130
I also learned that my kids
are a lot more resilient

00:15:41.130 --> 00:15:43.300
and a mature than I thought.

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:48.040
Jackson really stepped up
that week as a six-year-old

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:52.040
and as my oldest to really
take responsibility for himself

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:54.540
and help out as much as
he could with his brother.

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:55.970
Thank you for helping me.

00:15:55.970 --> 00:15:57.420
- You're welcome.

00:15:57.420 --> 00:16:01.590
- He's really empathetic and
so when I'm having a hard time,

00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:03.988
he really notices.

00:16:03.988 --> 00:16:06.772
- Sorry for not behaving.

00:16:06.772 --> 00:16:08.492
- I love you.

00:16:08.492 --> 00:16:10.140
- I love you too.

00:16:10.140 --> 00:16:12.730
- Sorry I'm having a hard time today.

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:13.888
- It's okay.

00:16:13.888 --> 00:16:15.000
- Okay.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:18.260
And last, I realized that
sometimes it's embarrassing to ask

00:16:18.260 --> 00:16:20.530
for help, and if you have a
single parent in your life,

00:16:20.530 --> 00:16:23.340
my suggestion would be to offer help.

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:26.880
I found myself not asking
for help so many times

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:28.920
throughout the week
because I felt embarrassed

00:16:28.920 --> 00:16:30.840
and I didn't want to burden people,

00:16:30.840 --> 00:16:33.610
and I feel like that
happens a lot with parents.

00:16:33.610 --> 00:16:37.180
It's up to us to proactively offer help,

00:16:37.180 --> 00:16:39.580
and find little things
that we can just do.

00:16:39.580 --> 00:16:42.640
Maybe it's just taking the
kids for an hour to the park

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:44.090
while the parent takes a nap.

00:16:44.090 --> 00:16:46.770
Just little things will
make all the difference,

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:49.043
and it's really on us
to help each other out.

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:52.470
So that's all I've got for today.

00:16:52.470 --> 00:16:54.550
I'm Hannah, and if you
are a single parent,

00:16:54.550 --> 00:16:55.720
let us know in the comments,

00:16:55.720 --> 00:16:57.330
and let us know what we can do to help.

00:16:57.330 --> 00:16:59.170
Or, if you know a single parent,

00:16:59.170 --> 00:17:00.850
or you were raised by a single parent,

00:17:00.850 --> 00:17:03.390
shout them out in the comments
or you can always text me.

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:05.790
This is a real number and I
do read every single text.

00:17:05.790 --> 00:17:08.330
Now, if you'll please excuse
me, I have a week's worth

00:17:08.330 --> 00:17:09.480
of laundry left to finish

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:12.080
and I feel like I'm gonna lose my mind.

00:17:12.080 --> 00:17:13.080
'Til next time!

00:17:13.080 --> 00:17:15.747
(playful music)

00:17:27.106 --> 00:17:29.480
Why is there a Christmas sweater in here?

00:17:29.480 --> 00:17:32.373
Nobody wore this!
(laughs)

